{
  "findings": [
    {
      "finding": "The level‑2 “paper’s quote” – *“The paper shows that vaccinating every newborn would eliminate tuberculosis within ten years.”* – does not appear anywhere in the article (provenance failure). It is presented as a paper‑derived statement but is actually a teaching choice (and it is unrelated to the “treatment and the threshold” theme of the level).",
      "kind": "unsupported",
      "level": 2,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Replace the quoted sentence with a genuine statement from the paper (e.g., the result that ℛ₀ < 1 when ℛᵥ < 1) or remove the quote entirely and re‑phrase the level description to refer to the paper’s actual finding about vaccination or treatment."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 4 fails the rule check and its “What‑if” (doubling the recovery rate α) only produces a faster flow into the recovered compartment – a purely definitional effect. The world therefore teaches nothing beyond the model’s built‑in mechanics.",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 4,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Change the What‑if to test a paper‑based insight, e.g., *“What if the treatment rate δ is increased instead of the recovery rate?*” – this would let learners explore how faster treatment reduces the infectious peak, which is discussed in the sensitivity analysis."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 5’s What‑if (“no one were treated → no one ever in the treated compartment”) is also purely definitional: setting δ = 0 forces T(t) ≡ 0 by the ODEs, so the level cannot illustrate any of the paper’s substantive results.",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 5,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Replace the What‑if with a scenario that varies the treatment rate δ to a low but non‑zero value and observes the resulting change in the infectious and treated compartments, which connects to the paper’s discussion of treatment impact on ℛᵥ."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 3 claims that “the epidemic doubles in size and the disease can never be controlled” when the progression rate γ is doubled. The paper’s measurement for this experiment is only a difference of six exposed individuals at year 10; the claim is far stronger than the evidence.",
      "kind": "overstated",
      "level": 3,
      "severity": "advice",
      "suggestion": "Re‑word the statement to match the measured outcome, e.g., *“With a two‑fold increase in the progression rate, the exposed population is six individuals higher at year 10, indicating a modest acceleration of the outbreak.”*"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 1’s quantitative claim – “with no vaccination, more than 5 more people are infectious at year 20 than in this world” – is not supported by any figure or table in the paper; the article never provides that specific number.",
      "kind": "unsupported",
      "level": 1,
      "severity": "advice",
      "suggestion": "Either remove the exact numeric claim or replace it with a qualitative statement that matches the paper’s sensitivity result (e.g., *“Removing vaccination increases the infectious population at year 20, as the sensitivity analysis shows a positive effect of π on ℛᵥ.”*)"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 7 asserts that halving the contact rate reduces the exposed peak by more than 1,000,000 people. The paper never reports such a magnitude; the only quantitative evidence is the sensitivity index for β, which does not translate into a concrete population‑level reduction of that size.",
      "kind": "overstated",
      "level": 7,
      "severity": "advice",
      "suggestion": "Adjust the claim to reflect the paper’s qualitative finding, e.g., *“Halving the contact rate noticeably lowers the exposed peak, consistent with the positive sensitivity of β on the reproduction number.”*"
    },
    {
      "finding": "The term “infectious rate” is used ambiguously in the world description (e.g., “infection rate (γ)” and “contact rate (β)”). In the paper β is the contact‑transmission coefficient, while γ is the progression rate from exposed to infectious. This wording can confuse learners.",
      "kind": "unsupported",
      "level": null,
      "severity": "advice",
      "suggestion": "Replace “infection rate” with “progression rate γ” and keep “contact rate β” for the transmission term, or add a brief glossary clarifying the two parameters."
    }
  ]
}
